英
文
摘
要
Facing the strategic needs of national cyberspace security and major public risk
prevention and control, and focusing on the key challenges posed by cross-modal
cognitive interference, lack of evidential reasoning, and strong domain dependency
in the AI era, this project pioneers a three-in-one theoretical system of "dynamic
evolution of evidence credibility - semantic consistency constraints 
cross-domain zero-shot generalization". It proposes an evidence perception-driven
theory based on temporal Bayesian graph neural networks, modeling text, images,
videos, and social feedback as multi-modal belief nodes to achieve a paradigm
shift from feature matching to causal reasoning. It innovatively proposes a
generalization technique combining joint distribution alignment and causal
intervention, which achieves zero-shot transfer between highly sensitive scenarios
without requiring labeled data from the target domain through adversarial style
stripping and invariant feature clustering. It establishes a three-level semantic
causal consistency constraint to achieve semantic-level collaborative reasoning
for text, images, and videos, effectively resisting cognitive falsification such
as "making things out of nothing" and "transposing flowers and grafting trees". By
integrating an edge lightweight detection module and a cloud-based traceability
engine, a closed-loop reasoning system is constructed that supports dynamic
evidence chain visualization and credibility heatmap output. With cognitive
reasoning as the core, causal stability as the support, and multi-modal
collaboration as the means, this project provides original theoretical support and
technical reference for building an interpretable, trustworthy, and generalizable
national cyberspace defense system.
